# pathtracer scene v1
#
# One subject, backlit, in amber glass.
#
#   Pathtracer --scene scenes/glass_dragon.pts --sky 0 --bounces 16 --samples 4096 \
#              --capture glass_dragon.png
#
# The dragon is dense flint with amber absorption, standing against a wall that is itself the
# light. Everything interesting here is Beer-Lambert: the tint is not a surface colour, it is
# what happens to light on its way through, so the thin parts of the mesh -- the wings, the
# tail, the edges of the jaw -- stay pale and the thick of the body goes deep orange and
# nearly opaque. A surface colour cannot do that, and a solid subject is the clearest place to
# see it. absorption_distance is 1.5 against a body about 3 units across, which puts the
# darkest part of the chest a couple of distances deep.
#
# The backdrop is an emissive slab rather than an area light, and the difference is the whole
# lighting design. An area light is not in the acceleration structure, so it can only ever be
# reached by a shadow ray -- and a shadow ray that crossed a transmissive surface from behind
# is discarded rather than trusted, because the straight line it assumed is not the path light
# takes through solid glass. An area light here would give a lit dragon with nothing behind it.
# The slab is real geometry, so a bounce ray can travel through the mesh and arrive at it,
# refracting properly on the way. That path is long -- floor, in, several internal reflections,
# out, backdrop -- so --bounces 16 is doing real work; drop it to 5 and the interior collapses.
#
# Being a `cube`, the slab is also sampled explicitly wherever that is any use, which is most
# of the frame: the floor and the near side of the dragon settle quickly. It is only the paths
# that arrive *through* the glass that fall back on chance, because the vertex before the light
# is a delta and there is no direction to connect toward. Those are the last thing to converge.
#
# The rim is cool and from the front left, purely to keep the silhouette from merging into the
# glow behind it. It is an ordinary area light; the dragon reads it two-sided because it is
# transmissive.
#
# Slow scene: the dragon is about 2.3M triangles. Trim overall brightness with --exposure.

camera
  position 0.9 1.9 7.4
  yaw 186
  pitch -6
  fov 36
  aperture 0.02
  focus_distance 7.2

entity "floor"
  shape       cube
  translation 0 -0.1 0
  rotation    0 0 0
  scale       8 0.1 8
  albedo      0.12 0.12 0.13
  roughness   0.3

entity "dragon"
  shape        dragon
  translation  0 1.29 0
  rotation     -90 25 0
  scale        1.9 1.9 1.9
  albedo       1 1 1
  roughness    0
  transmission 1
  ior          1.785
  abbe         26
  absorption   0.85 0.45 0.1
  absorption_distance 1.1

# The light, and the background, and the thing the glass is showing you. Black albedo: it
# emits, it does not also bounce.
entity "backdrop"
  shape             cube
  translation       0 3.5 -4.5
  rotation          0 0 0
  scale             9 3.5 0.05
  albedo            0 0 0
  emission          1 0.93 0.82
  emission_strength 1.5
  roughness         1

light "rim"
  type      area
  position  -4 3.2 2.2
  direction 0.8 -0.35 -0.5
  color     0.7 0.8 1
  intensity 6
  size      1.2 1.2
